<comment datatype='string'>&lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=GLUTARYL-COA&quot;&gt;Glutaryl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; is a common intermediate in the degradation of many different compounds,
such as &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=BENZOATE&quot;&gt;benzoate&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=BENZOYLCOA&quot;&gt;benzoyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=LYS&quot;&gt;L-lysine&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=TRP&quot;&gt;L-tryptophan&lt;/a&gt; and many more.
In this pathway &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=GLUTARYL-COA&quot;&gt;glutaryl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; is degraded by &amp;beta;-oxidation to 2 molecules of
&lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETYL-COA&quot;&gt;acetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; and one molecule of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CARBON-DIOXIDE&quot;&gt;CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;p&gt;
This pathway can operate under both anaerobic [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8436125&quot;&gt;Koch93&lt;/a&gt;] and aerobic conditions
[&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/11705404&quot;&gt;Westover01&lt;/a&gt;], although the enzymes catalyzing it may be specialized for either aerobic
or anaerobic operation.</comment>
